# 2. How Windows Event Logging Works

Windows uses an event infrastructure in which applications, operating system components, and security providers generate structured events.

A simplified architecture is:

```text
+------------------------------------+
| Applications and Windows Services  |
+------------------------------------+
                  |
                  v
+------------------------------------+
| Event Providers                    |
|                                    |
| Security, System, Defender, etc.   |
+------------------------------------+
                  |
                  v
+------------------------------------+
| Windows Event Infrastructure       |
+------------------------------------+
                  |
                  v
+------------------------------------+
| Event Channels / Logs               |
|                                    |
| Security, System, Application, etc.|
+------------------------------------+
                  |
                  v
+------------------------------------+
| Event Viewer / PowerShell / SIEM    |
+------------------------------------+
                  |
                  v
+------------------------------------+
| Administrator / SOC Analyst        |
+------------------------------------+
```

This is a conceptual diagram, not a complete description of the internal Windows Event Log architecture.

## 2.1 Event providers

An event provider is a component that generates events.

Examples include:
- Windows Security auditing providers.
- Microsoft Defender Antivirus.
- Windows Service Control Manager.
- Application-specific logging providers.
- Kernel and networking components.

Each provider can generate different types of events.

For example, Microsoft Defender can generate events when it detects a threat, while the Service Control Manager can generate events about services starting or stopping.

## 2.2 Event channels

A channel is a logical destination for events.

Examples include:
- `Security`
- `System`
- `Application`
- `Microsoft-Windows-Windows Defender/Operational`

Different channels serve different purposes. Some record general operating system activity, while others provide detailed information about particular components.

## 2.3 Event records

An event record contains structured information about an occurrence.

A typical event may include:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Provider | Component that generated the event. |
| Event ID | Identifier for the event type within the provider's event definitions. |
| Level | Severity or importance, such as Information, Warning, or Error. |
| TimeCreated | Time the event was recorded. |
| Computer | Computer on which the event was recorded. |
| User or security context | User or account associated with the event, when applicable. |
| Event data | Additional fields describing the occurrence. |
| Record ID | Identifier for the event record within its log. |

Do not confuse Event ID with Record ID. Event ID describes the type of event; Record ID identifies a particular record in a log.

For example, many failed logons can produce different event records with Event ID 4625.

