# 6. Important Windows Security Event IDs

Event IDs are among the most important concepts for Windows security monitoring.

They help analysts locate specific categories of activity in Windows event logs.

## 6.1 Authentication and logon events

| Event ID | Meaning | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| 4624 | Successful logon | Identify successful access to a system. |
| 4625 | Failed logon | Investigate repeated failures and potential brute-force attempts. |
| 4634 | Account logged off | Understand session termination. |
| 4647 | User initiated logoff | Identify user-initiated session endings. |
| 4648 | Logon attempted using explicit credentials | Investigate processes or users supplying alternate credentials. |
| 4672 | Special privileges assigned to a new logon | Identify sessions granted sensitive privileges. |
| 4771 | Kerberos pre-authentication failed | Investigate certain Kerberos authentication failures. |
| 4776 | Domain controller attempted to validate credentials using NTLM | Investigate NTLM authentication activity in applicable environments. |

These events are not interchangeable. For example, Event ID 4672 does not automatically indicate malicious privilege escalation; it can occur during normal administrative logons.

## 6.2 Account and privilege changes

| Event ID | Meaning | Why it matters |
|---|---|---|
| 4720 | User account created | Investigate unexpected account creation. |
| 4722 | User account enabled | Detect unexpected reactivation of accounts. |
| 4724 | Attempt to reset an account password | Investigate unauthorized password-reset activity. |
| 4728 | Member added to a security-enabled global group | Investigate privileged group changes in applicable environments. |
| 4732 | Member added to a security-enabled local group | Investigate local group membership changes. |
| 4738 | User account changed | Investigate modifications to user accounts. |
| 4725 | User account disabled | Investigate unexpected account disablement. |
| 1102 | Security audit log cleared | Investigate possible evidence destruction or administrative maintenance. |

For example, Event ID 4732 may be important when an unexpected user is added to the local Administrators group.

Remember that these events are generated when the relevant auditing is enabled and the corresponding action is covered by that auditing configuration.

## 6.3 Process execution and persistence

| Event ID | Meaning | Investigation value |
|---|---|---|
| 4688 | A new process was created | Investigate process execution when process-creation auditing is enabled. |
| 4697 | A service was installed | Investigate unexpected service installation when the applicable auditing is enabled. |
| 4698 | A scheduled task was created | Investigate potential persistence through scheduled tasks. |
| 4702 | A scheduled task was updated | Investigate changes to existing tasks. |

These events are particularly useful when investigating suspicious programs and persistence mechanisms.

However, Event ID 4688 may not be enabled by default in a given environment. Command-line information also requires the relevant additional policy configuration.

### Example: Suspicious process execution

Imagine a workstation records the following sequence:

```text
09:14 — User opens an unexpected attachment.
09:15 — A scripting process starts.
09:15 — A second process launches from a temporary directory.
09:16 — A scheduled task is created.
09:17 — The computer makes an unusual outbound connection.
```

A SOC analyst might correlate:
- Event ID 4688, if process auditing is enabled.
- Event ID 4698, if the task-creation event is audited.
- Relevant network telemetry.
- Microsoft Defender detections.
- PowerShell logs, if available.

The objective is to determine whether the events form a coherent attack sequence rather than treating each event independently.

## 6.4 System and logging events

| Event ID | Meaning | Investigation value |
|---|---|---|
| 6005 | Event Log service started | Help establish system logging startup activity. |
| 6006 | Event Log service stopped | Help investigate shutdown or service activity. |
| 6008 | Previous system shutdown was unexpected | Investigate unexpected shutdowns. |
| 1102 | Security audit log cleared | Investigate potential loss of security evidence. |

Event IDs must always be interpreted in the context of their provider and log. The same numeric ID can have different meanings in different event providers.

For more detail on event querying, consult Microsoft's official [`Get-WinEvent` documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent). [Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent?view=powershell-5.1\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)

