# 9. Check Microsoft Defender's Status

You should learn how to inspect Defender before investigating its detections.

## 9.1 Open Windows Security

On Windows:

1. Open the Start menu.
2. Search for Windows Security.
3. Open the application.
4. Select Virus & threat protection.

Depending on your system, this interface may show:
- Current threat status.
- Scan options.
- Protection settings.
- Protection history.
- Security provider information.

Do not assume that seeing Windows Security installed means every protection feature is enabled.

## 9.2 Check Defender using PowerShell

Open PowerShell and run:

```powershell
Get-MpComputerStatus
```

This returns Defender status information, including properties related to:
- Antivirus enablement.
- Real-time protection.
- Antispyware protection.
- Antivirus signatures.
- Recent scan information.

To inspect a smaller selection of properties:

```powershell
Get-MpComputerStatus |
    Select-Object AntivirusEnabled,
                  RealTimeProtectionEnabled,
                  AMServiceEnabled,
                  AntivirusSignatureLastUpdated
```

Property availability can vary across versions and environments.

## 9.3 Check protection preferences

```powershell
Get-MpPreference
```

This displays configured Microsoft Defender preferences.

You can use it to inspect settings such as exclusions and scan preferences.

Security warning: Exclusions can create blind spots. An overly broad exclusion may prevent files or locations from receiving expected antivirus scanning. Do not add exclusions just to make a suspicious program run.

## 9.4 Update Defender security intelligence

On a personal or authorised lab computer, you can request a security intelligence update:

```powershell
Update-MpSignature
```

This asks Microsoft Defender to update its security intelligence using the configured update mechanism.

You can then check the update timestamp:

```powershell
Get-MpComputerStatus |
    Select-Object AntivirusSignatureLastUpdated
```

If your device is managed by an organisation, its security policies may control update behavior.

## 9.5 Run an antivirus scan

To run a quick scan:

```powershell
Start-MpScan -ScanType QuickScan
```

To request a full scan:

```powershell
Start-MpScan -ScanType FullScan
```

A quick scan checks common locations and other areas selected by Defender. A full scan performs a broader examination of files and running programs.

Scan duration depends on the device, storage, and amount of data.

