# 10. Defender Event Logs and Threat Investigation

Microsoft Defender records operational information in Windows Event Logs.

The principal channel to examine is:

```text
Microsoft-Windows-Windows Defender/Operational
```

### Open it in Event Viewer

Navigate to:

```text
Event Viewer
    → Applications and Services Logs
        → Microsoft
            → Windows
                → Windows Defender
                    → Operational
```

This log can provide evidence about Defender scans, detections, protection changes, and other operational activity. [Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-endpoint/troubleshoot-mdav-scan-issues?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

## 10.1 Read Defender events using PowerShell

First, retrieve recent events:

```powershell
Get-WinEvent `
    -LogName 'Microsoft-Windows-Windows Defender/Operational' `
    -MaxEvents 30 |
    Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, LevelDisplayName, Message
```

To investigate scan-related events, filter by Event ID 1000:

```powershell
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{
    LogName = 'Microsoft-Windows-Windows Defender/Operational'
    Id      = 1000
} -MaxEvents 20 |
    Select-Object TimeCreated, Id, Message
```

Event ID 1000 is associated with a scan starting in the documented Defender event-log examples. Interpret it using its message and surrounding records rather than assuming every event in the channel is a threat detection. [Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-endpoint/troubleshoot-mdav-scan-issues?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

## 10.2 Important Defender Event IDs

The following are useful starting points when investigating Defender activity:

| Event ID | General meaning | Why you would inspect it |
|---|---|---|
| 1000 | Scan started | Establish when a scan began. |
| 1001 | Scan completed | Determine whether a scan completed. |
| 1116 | Malware or potentially unwanted software detected | Investigate a detection and its associated threat details. |
| 1117 | An action was taken in response to a detection | Investigate the protection response. |
| 1118 | A protection action encountered an error | Determine why an action did not complete successfully. |
| 5001 | Real-time protection disabled | Investigate unexpected changes to protection. |
| 5007 | Microsoft Defender configuration changed | Examine the configuration change and its context. |

Event details can vary by platform and version. Verify the event's provider, message, and structured data on your own system before drawing conclusions.

A detection event does not automatically mean the device is currently infected. The threat may have been blocked or quarantined, or the detection may require additional investigation.

## 10.3 Investigate a malware detection

Suppose Microsoft Defender detects a suspicious file.

A sensible investigation process is:

Step 1 — Identify the detection

Review the event message, threat name, timestamp, and affected resource.

Step 2 — Check the response

Determine whether Defender blocked the activity, quarantined the file, or successfully remediated the threat.

Step 3 — Inspect Protection History

Open Windows Security and review the associated detection and action.

Step 4 — Correlate other evidence

Look for related events, such as:
- Process creation.
- Unusual PowerShell activity.
- New scheduled tasks.
- Unexpected services.
- Suspicious network connections.

Step 5 — Determine the impact

Assess whether the threat executed, whether remediation succeeded, and whether other files or accounts might be affected.

Step 6 — Escalate or contain when necessary

On an organisational device, follow the incident-response process. Do not delete evidence or change security settings before preserving information needed for investigation.

